<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Avanti</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.) · capital Ujjain</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Malwa with its capital at Ujjain, ruled by the fierce Chanda Pradyota and one of the four great powers of the Buddha's time.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Avanti occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> plateau of western central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahishmati">Mahishmati</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> in the south. By the 6th century BCE it was one of the four leading kingdoms alongside <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kosala</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-vatsa">Vatsa</a>. Puranic tradition says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pradyota-dynasty">Pradyota dynasty</a> seized the throne after the last king of an older line was killed by his minister Pulika.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pradyota">Chanda Pradyota</a> Mahasena, a contemporary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>, was remembered as violent and ambitious; he threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a> and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayana-of-vatsa">Udayana of Vatsa</a>, who eloped with his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasavadatta">Vasavadatta</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> grew rich on the routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> with the western ports, and Avanti later became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> through the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakaccana">Mahakaccana</a>, a native of Ujjain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 400 BCE Avanti was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha</a>, ending its independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> remained a provincial capital under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, and later became the seat of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-avanti.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Avanti" width="320" height="246"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahajanapadas_(c._500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ujjain</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chanda Pradyota, Palaka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Avanti, Pradyota dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=kingdom-of-avanti" aria-label="Kingdom of Avanti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6703 1323 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Avanti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7023 1579L7021 1385M7023 1579L7266 1513"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7021 1385h0M7266 1513h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7023 1579h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7023 1579h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ujjain. <a href="/atla